.\" This file written by AI :)
.\" Man page for ttype.
.TH TTYPE 1 "September 2026" "ttype" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
ttype \- terminal typing practice
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B ttype
.RI [ flags ]
.br
.B ttype
.I command
.RI [ flags ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B ttype
is a typing test that runs entirely in the terminal. It measures words per
minute, raw speed, accuracy and consistency, keeps a local history of every
run, and can play a finished run back keystroke by keystroke.
.PP
With no arguments it starts a 60 second timed test using the saved defaults.
.SH TEST FLAGS
.TP
.BI \-\-time " N"
Run a timed test of N seconds. Default 60.
.TP
.BI \-\-words " N"
Run until N words are finished instead of against the clock.
.TP
.BI \-\-mode " MODE"
Text to type: \fBwords\fR, \fBsentences\fR, \fBsql\fR, \fBgo\fR,
\fBbackend\fR, \fBpython\fR, \fBshell\fR or \fBregex\fR.
In the word modes space commits the current word; the code modes keep spaces
literal.
.TP
.BI \-\-language " ID"
Word list to sample from, for example \fBspanish\fR or \fBenglish_1k\fR.
Downloaded once and cached locally.
.TP
.BI \-\-theme " NAME"
Colour theme: \fBdefault\fR, \fBmonokai\fR or \fBdracula\fR.
.TP
.B \-\-punctuation
Inject punctuation between words.
.TP
.B \-\-numbers
Inject numbers between words.
.TP
.B \-\-blind
Hide correctness while typing. The live line shows raw speed only.
.TP
.B \-\-zen
Words and nothing else: no header, no hints, no footer.
.TP
.BI \-\-min\-wpm " N"
Fail the run if the pace drops below N after a five second grace period.
.TP
.BI \-\-width " N"
Typing area width in columns. 0 follows the terminal, capped at 100.
.TP
.BI \-\-seed " N"
Seed the generator, so the same seed always produces the same text.
.TP
.BI \-\-output " json"
Print the finished result as JSON instead of showing the result screen.
.TP
.BI \-\-file " PATH"
Type the text of a file instead of generated text;
.B \-
reads standard input. Text piped into
.B ttype
is used the same way.
.TP
.BI \-\-text " TEXT"
Type the given text.
.TP
.B \-\-no\-save
Keep the run out of history, personal bests and replays.
.TP
.BI \-\-result\-file " PATH"
When ttype exits, write the last run to PATH as JSON: its status
.RB ( completed ,
.B quit
or
.BR failed_min_wpm ),
the numbers, the words missed and what was typed, and for custom text a SHA-256
of it and the line each word came from.
.TP
.B \-\-allow\-skip
Deprecated and ignored. Space already commits the word.
.SH CUSTOM TEXT
Text given with
.BR \-\-file ,
.B \-\-text
or a pipe is typed whole, unless
.B \-\-words
or
.B \-\-time
shortens it; a timed test also ends when the text does. Newlines and tabs
become spaces, colour codes are removed, and typographic quotes, dashes and
ellipses become ASCII. It cannot be combined with
.BR \-\-mode ,
.BR \-\-language ,
.B \-\-punctuation
or
.BR \-\-numbers .
Custom runs are listed under the mode
.B custom
and never count as personal bests. A replay stores the text, so use
.B \-\-no\-save
for anything private.
.SH COMMANDS
.TP
.B history [\-\-last N] [\-\-plain]
List past results. On a terminal this is an interactive table where
.B enter
plays the selected run back;
.B \-\-plain
prints a plain table instead.
.TP
.B stats [\-\-mode M] [\-\-exclude\-failed] [\-\-trend] [\-\-export csv]
Averages, personal bests and an optional sparkline of wpm over time.
.B \-\-export csv
writes the history to standard output instead.
.TP
.B config [\-\-default\-* ...] [\-\-update auto|notify|off]
Print the saved defaults, or change the ones you name.
.B \-\-update
sets what happens with new releases:
.B auto
installs them in the background (the default),
.B notify
only marks them in the footer, and
.B off
never checks.
.TP
.B languages [download]
List cached word lists, or fetch every available list.
.TP
.B clear [history|languages|all] [\-\-yes]
Delete saved data. Asks for confirmation unless
.B \-\-yes
is given.
.TP
.B doctor
Check the terminal size, colour support, locale, clipboard tool and data
directory, with a hint for anything that fails.
.TP
.B update
Download and install the latest release now, verifying its checksum first.
A copy installed by Homebrew, Scoop, a system package manager or
.B go install
is left alone, and the command to update it is printed instead.
.IP
Without it, ttype looks for a new release at most once a day, in the
background. When it finds one it can install, it downloads it, verifies the
checksum, runs the new binary with
.B \-\-version
and swaps it in; the new version runs from the next launch. It does not
install a new major version, into a directory it cannot write, or on Windows;
the footer marks those and
.B u
on the result screen names the command. See
.B config \-\-update
and
.BR TTYPE_UPDATE .
.TP
.B completion [bash|zsh|fish|powershell]
Print a completion script for the given shell.
.SH KEYS
.SS While typing
.TP
.B backspace
Delete one character.
.TP
.B ctrl+backspace, ctrl+w, alt+backspace
Delete the word before the cursor, along with the space after it.
.TP
.B space
Commit the current word and move on, skipping whatever was left of it.
.TP
.B ctrl+o
Show or hide the live stats line.
.TP
.B ctrl+s
Open the settings panel without leaving the run.
.TP
.B tab, enter
Restart with fresh text.
.TP
.B ?
Open the help overlay. Only before the first keystroke; once a run is under
way it is typed input.
.TP
.B esc, ctrl+c
Quit.
.SS On the result screen
.TP
.B tab, enter, r
Restart.
.TP
.B p
Watch the replay of the run.
.TP
.B C
Copy the result line to the clipboard.
.TP
.B S
Settings.
.TP
.B M
Mode picker.
.TP
.B L
Language picker.
.TP
.B u
Install a newer version now, or say what to run when ttype cannot.
.TP
.B q, esc
Quit.
.SS Replay playback
.TP
.B space
Pause and resume.
.TP
.B 1, 2, 4
Playback speed.
.TP
.B r
Start the replay over.
.TP
.B esc, q
Back to the result screen or the history table.
.SH STATISTICS
.TP
.B wpm
Correct characters divided by five, over the elapsed time.
.TP
.B raw
Every character typed, right or wrong, over the same time.
.TP
.B accuracy
Correct keystrokes as they were typed. Correcting a mistake does not restore
it.
.TP
.B consistency
How even the per-second raw speed was, on a 0 to 100 scale.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I ~/.config/ttype/config.json
Saved defaults.
.TP
.I ~/.local/share/ttype/history.json
Test history, capped at the newest 1000 runs.
.TP
.I ~/.local/share/ttype/bests.json
Personal bests.
.TP
.I ~/.local/share/ttype/replays/
Recorded runs, newest 50 kept.
.TP
.I ~/.local/share/ttype/languages/
Downloaded word lists.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B XDG_CONFIG_HOME
Overrides the config directory.
.TP
.B XDG_DATA_HOME
Overrides the data directory.
.TP
.B NO_COLOR
Disables colour output.
.TP
.B TTYPE_BIDI
Who puts right-to-left text (Persian, Arabic, Hebrew) in display order:
.B ttype
or the
.BR terminal .
By default ttype does it, unless the terminal is built on VTE, or is Konsole
or mlterm, which reorder text themselves.
.TP
.B TTYPE_UPDATE
.BR auto ,
.B notify
or
.BR off ;
overrides
.B config \-\-update
for this run. With
.B CI
set, or with
.BR "\-\-output json" ,
ttype never checks for updates.
.SH EXAMPLES
.TP
A quick sprint:
.B ttype \-\-time 15
.TP
Fifty words of Go with the numbers on:
.B ttype \-\-words 50 \-\-mode go \-\-numbers
.TP
The same text as a friend:
.B ttype \-\-seed 4242
.TP
Practise on your own notes without saving them:
.B ttype \-\-file notes.txt \-\-no\-save
.TP
Export everything to a spreadsheet:
.B ttype stats \-\-export csv > runs.csv
.SH SEE ALSO
Project page: https://github.com/alirezaudev/ttype
